      "type": "about",
      "tabLabel": "About",
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Tsung Shap",
          "subtitle": "Card Game",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Tsung Shap (Disputing Tens) is a Chinese domino game adapted for cards. 2 players capture pairs of identical cards and sets of 3 cards summing to multiples of 10 from the ends of a row. Pairs score 10x pip value. Sweeps score 40 bonus. Can be played with standard card deck using Fishing mechanics."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Tsung Shap was reported by Stewart Culin in Chinese Games With Dice And Dominoes (1895), describing games played by Chinese labourers in the USA. The name means disputing tens. It uses one set of 32 Chinese dominoes as a two-player fishing game, with tiles in a row and captures from the ends."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Setup",
          "subtitle": "Table, deck, equipment, and deal",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Two or more players."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "32 tiles/cards. Arrange in 8 stacks of 4."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Tiles or cards"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Players draw stacks alternately. 16 each."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
